{"type": "song", "artist": "Lil Uzi Vert", "song": "Skir Skirr", "status": "done", "targets": 15, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Lil-uzi-vert-skir-skirr-lyrics", "genius_title": "Skir Skirr", "genius_id": 3254442, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Uzi Vert", "song": "Six Four", "line": "She work that dick like a 4-stroke Stand on the seat, and do tricks ho She was broke, but how i kept her All of my niggas they kick dogs", "vault_lines": ["She work that dick like a 4-stroke"], "meaning": "1) The term “4-stroke” functions as a wild hyperbole for a multi-cylinder engine block. Uzi implies the girl handles sexual acts with extreme mechanical efficiency, just like an engine block orchestrates four cylinders firing at once.\n\n2) The follow-up line, “Stand on the seat, and do tricks ho,” completely shifts the tone into a degrading, dominant command—resembling a master training a dog. Uzi orders her to get into position and perform explicit sexual acts (“do tricks”, i.e., perform oral sex) on command. By treating her skills less like a mutual encounter and more like a trained circus act or an obedience routine, Uzi asserts absolute authority and status, reducing her role to pure entertainment for the artist.\n\n3) “She was broke, but how i kept her”: Uzi reinforces the pet dynamic by using the word “kept”. In street and financial slang, to “keep” someone means to fully support them financially. However, in this specific context, it doubles as “keeping a dog\"—Uzi rescued her from poverty (\"she was broke”) and now provides shelter, food, and luxury, housing her like an expensive, dependent pet.\n\n4) “All of my niggas they kick dogs”: This final line serves as a brutal street warning and a display of zero emotional attachment. While Uzi “keeps” her, he makes it clear that his circle has no sympathy for “dogs” (promiscuous women/thots). “Kicking dogs” is a raw, violent metaphor meaning his crew treats these women with zero respect, using them and throwing them out the moment they act up or stop obeying. It highlights the ultimate danger of her position: she is completely disposable to Uzi and his team.", "annotation_id": "40263291", "url": "https://genius.com/40263291", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Uzi Vert", "song": "Six Four", "line": "She gave slop slop, that’s a [?]", "vault_lines": ["She gave slop slop, that's a [?]"], "meaning": "The term “slop slop” is explicit onomatopoeic slang for oral sex, mimicking the sound. Uzi uses this crude phrasing to detach themselves emotionally from the situation, treating the encounter as purely casual and transactional.", "annotation_id": "40263245", "url": "https://genius.com/40263245", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Uzi Vert", "song": "Six Four", "line": "She made the block hot, that’s thot thot", "vault_lines": ["She made the block hot, that's thot thot"], "meaning": "Uzi uses a raw street idiom where “made the block hot” doesn’t just mean bringing police attention, but rather refers to a girl sleeping around with everyone in the neighborhood. While the acronym “THOT” (“That Hoe Over There”) has long been standard rap culture slang for a promiscuous woman, Uzi’s wordplay “thot thot” functions as an announcement: “that specific thot is officially here on the block.” It emphasizes her notorious reputation across the entire area.", "annotation_id": "40263241", "url": "https://genius.com/40263241", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Uzi Vert", "song": "Six Four", "line": "No I do not got no uber What the fuck you think is lift up", "vault_lines": ["No I do not got no uber"], "meaning": "This line features a clever double entendre using popular rideshare brands. Uzi states they never use Uber. Instead, they play on the name of its biggest competitor, Lyft (which sounds identical to lift). In this context, “lift up” refers to the butterfly or scissor doors on their ultra-luxury hypercars (like a Lamborghini Aventador or McLaren) that open vertically upward.", "annotation_id": "40263185", "url": "https://genius.com/40263185", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Lil Uzi Vert", "song": "Six Four", "line": "Turbo my whip like a Nismo", "vault_lines": ["Turbo my whip like a Nismo"], "meaning": "Nismo (short for Nissan Motorsport) is the iconic racing and tuning division of Nissan. Vehicles with the Nismo badge are upgraded with performance turbochargers, sport-tuned suspensions, and massive horsepower. Uzi adopts this as a metaphor for their lightning-fast lifestyle, elite supercar collection, and rapid career momentum.", "annotation_id": "40263180", "url": "https://genius.com/40263180", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
